Publish the UST-10LX's "no echo" as NaN, not as a 65.533 m range.

The lidar reports no echo as 65533 mm, and upstream publishes that as a
range. It never changes as the car moves, so scan matching reads it as
"not moving": rf2o failed on 30 % of the scans outdoors. NaN means "no
return" to every consumer in this stack.

Upstream: https://github.com/Hokuyo-aut/urg_node2 at f02d8af
License:  Apache-2.0, as urg_node2

diff --git a/src/urg_node2.cpp b/src/urg_node2.cpp
index 36e3214..f7d0cf1 100644
--- a/src/urg_node2.cpp
+++ b/src/urg_node2.cpp
@@ -569,8 +569,13 @@ bool UrgNode2::create_scan_message(sensor_msgs::msg::LaserScan & msg)
   }
 
   for (int i = 0; i < num_beams; i++) {
-    if (distance_[i] != 0) {
-      msg.ranges[i] = static_cast<float>(distance_[i]) / 1000.0;
+    const float range = static_cast<float>(distance_[i]) / 1000.0;
+    // A reading past the sensor's own maximum is an error code, not a
+    // distance: the UST-10LX sends 65533 for "no echo". Published as 65.533 m
+    // it is a finite range that never changes, which scan matching reads as
+    // "not moving" - a run of them makes rf2o divide by zero.
+    if (distance_[i] != 0 && range <= msg.range_max) {
+      msg.ranges[i] = range;
       if (use_intensity_) {
         msg.intensities[i] = intensity_[i];
       }
